# A copy and not a fork. Nothing here is changed.
#
# A wheel has no commit, so the record above is the file and its checksum. It
# was read from the JSON of PyPI at the moment of the copy and compared with
# the bytes, and tests/test_dbus_next.py compares the tree against them again.
#
# Why it is carried at all:
#
# Three services of cec-toolkit import this module at their first line. It
# belongs neither to this project nor to SteamOS, and SteamOS ships no pip,
# so there is no one command that installs it. The toolkit's installer only
# warns about it.
#
# The copy that a person installs by hand lands in the user site directory,
# whose name holds the version of Python: .local/lib/python3.14/site-packages.
# A SteamOS update that raises Python leaves it behind, and the three services
# then die at their first line for ever. Each of their units carries
# Restart=on-failure, so they stay in "activating" and never reach "failed",
# and every switch on the page keeps saying "on".
#
# That was measured on a machine: SteamOS went from Python 3.13 to 3.14 and
# all three stopped. See server/steamos_utility_center/cec.py, NEEDS_DBUS.
#
# So the installer puts this tree in /var/lib/steamos-utility-center/python.
# /var is its own partition and survives an update, and that path holds no
# version of Python. The module is pure Python with no compiled part, so one
# copy serves every version of Python 3.
#
# Taking a newer version:
#
#   read the wheel URL and its sha256 from https://pypi.org/pypi/dbus-next/json
#   unpack dbus_next/ and the LICENSE of the dist-info over this directory
#   write the version into VERSION and the four values above
#
